        <h2>Day Trips from Madiha</h2>
        <p><a href="/places/mirissa">Mirissa</a> is 8km west — 15 minutes by tuk-tuk — and the departure point for <a href="/things-to-do/whale-watching">whale watching</a> trips between November and April. Blue whales, sperm whales and spinner dolphin pods are all possible. Book a morning trip (depart 6–6:30am) the day before through your guesthouse.</p>
        <p>Matara — the major city at the southern tip of the expressway — is 4km east. It has good food, a Dutch fort of its own (smaller and less visited than Galle), and the southern terminus of the coastal railway. The Dondra Head Lighthouse at Sri Lanka's southernmost point is a 10-minute drive east of Matara; this is as far south as Sri Lanka goes, and the lighthouse perch above the cliffs gives a perspective on the island's geography that's hard to find elsewhere.</p>
        <p><a href="/places/galle">Galle Fort</a> is about 35km west — a one-hour tuk-tuk or 45-minute bus — and the most rewarding day trip from the Madiha area. The fort's cobbled streets, boutique hotels, galleries and rampart sunset walk are incomparable.</p>

        <h2>Getting to Madiha</h2>
        <p>The nearest railway station is Matara, 4km east. The Colombo–Matara coastal train (around 3.5 hours from Colombo Fort) is one of the most scenic in Asia; from Matara, a tuk-tuk to Madiha costs LKR 300–400 and takes around 10 minutes. Buses along the A2 coastal road between Galle and Matara pass through Madiha — ask to be dropped at the beach road. From Colombo by car via the Southern Expressway, allow 2–2.5 hours to Matara, then a short drive west along the coast.</p>

      </article>

